# The Robocode model

**Topic 01: A first robot**

After this talk you can say what happens in one tick, which of your methods the engine calls, and why Hadur keeps its brain in `static` fields.

Java skills: classes, inheritance, overriding, `static` versus instance.

---

# A battle is rounds, a round is ticks

- A **battle** is a number of **rounds**. A round ends when one robot is left.
- A round is a series of **ticks**, also called turns.
- On each tick the engine lets every robot act once, then moves bullets and robots.

Your robot is a Java class. The engine creates it, starts a thread, and calls `run()`.

---

# A robot is a class that extends AdvancedRobot

```java
public class Tiny extends AdvancedRobot {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            setTurnRadarRightRadians(Double.POSITIVE_INFINITY);
            execute();
        }
    }
}
```

`extends` gives you everything `AdvancedRobot` already does. `@Override` says that `run()` replaces the version in the parent.

This sample is ours, not Hadur's. Hadur's `run()` is the same shape.

---

# Setters queue, execute() acts

`setAhead(100)` does not move the robot. It writes down an order.

`execute()` ends your turn. The engine then carries out every queued order for one tick and comes back.

Hadur applies its orders in one place:

```java
if (!Double.isNaN(o.maxVelocity())) setMaxVelocity(o.maxVelocity());
if (!Double.isNaN(o.bodyTurn())) setTurnRightRadians(o.bodyTurn());
if (!Double.isNaN(o.ahead())) setAhead(o.ahead());
// ...
if (o.firePower() > 0) setFire(o.firePower());
```

*Source: `hadur2.Hadur.apply`.*

---

# Events are methods the engine calls

You override a handler and the engine calls it when something happens.

```java
@Override
public void onScannedRobot(ScannedRobotEvent e) {
    pending.add(new BotEvent.Scan(e.getName(), e.getBearingRadians(), e.getDistance(),
        e.getEnergy(), e.getHeadingRadians(), e.getVelocity(), e.isSentryRobot()));
}
```

*Source: `hadur2.Hadur.onScannedRobot`.*

Handlers run during `execute()`, in the engine's own priority order. Hadur does almost nothing inside them. It turns the event into a value and queues it.

---

# One tick, as Hadur sees it

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    X["execute()"] --> H["engine calls handlers<br/>onScannedRobot, onHitByBullet, ..."]
    H --> Q["handlers queue events"]
    Q --> L["run() loop: read getters,<br/>build input, get orders"]
    L --> S["setters queue orders"]
    S --> X
```

Everything the robot knows arrives by getters and handlers. Everything it does leaves by setters.

---

# Coordinates and headings

- The origin is the **bottom-left** corner. `y` grows upward.
- Heading 0 is **north**. Angles grow **clockwise**, so east is a quarter turn.
- A **bearing** is relative to your own heading. A heading is absolute.

Hadur's `BattleField` documents the maths:

```java
 * and angles are radians with 0 north and growing clockwise, so a point at angle {@code a}
 * and distance {@code d} from {@code p} is {@code (p.x + sin(a) d, p.y + cos(a) d)}.</p>
```

The absolute direction of a scanned enemy is `heading + bearing`. Hadur's guard does exactly that.

---

# Turn limits come from the rules

| Part | Turn rate |
|---|---|
| Body | 10 degrees per tick, less 0.75 per px/tick of speed |
| Gun | 20 degrees per tick, on top of the body |
| Radar | 45 degrees per tick, on top of the gun |

Hadur sets two flags so the three turn independently:

```java
setAdjustGunForRobotTurn(true);
setAdjustRadarForGunTurn(true);
```

*Sources: `hadur2.core.physics.Rules`, `hadur2.Hadur.run`.*

---

# New object each round, so use static

The engine makes a **new robot object** every round. Instance fields start empty each time.

Anything the robot must remember across rounds goes in `static` fields:

```java
/** The brain for the whole battle; built on the first round's {@code run()}. */
private static HadurCore core;
/** Wraps every core tick (RES-1); lives as long as the core. */
private static Guard guard;
```

Per-round state, such as the queue of pending events, stays an instance field.

---

# Hadur's loop, in full

```java
while (true) {
    BotInput in = input();
    // ...
    BotOrders orders = guard.tick(in);
    // ...
    apply(orders);
    execute();
}
```

Build the input, ask the brain, apply the orders, end the turn. Lab 01 builds this loop in miniature.

*Source: `hadur2.Hadur.run`, with comments and timing code left out.*

---

# Recap

- A robot extends `AdvancedRobot`; the engine calls `run()` and your handlers.
- Setters queue; `execute()` ends the turn.
- Headings: 0 is north, clockwise, radians.
- New robot object every round, so long-lived state is `static`.

Next: the lab, then the reading note on Hadur's adapter.
